{{wikipedia|Chad (paper)}}
[[File:Punch card chads2.mw.jpg|thumb|Mounds of chads generated from [[punch card]]s]]

===Etymology===
Uncertain; possibly from the English slang term {{m|en|chat||[[louse]]}}. The word predates the [[Chadless punch]], which therefore cannot be its origin,<ref>{{cite-web|author=David P. Mikkelson|title=Chad: Does the word ‘chad’ come from the Chadless keypunch, invented by a Mr. Chadless?|url=http://www.snopes.com/business/names/chad.asp|archiveurl=<!--Cannot be archived by Archive.org as "[t]his URL has been excluded from the Wayback Machine"-->|archivedate=|work={{w|Snopes.com}}|date=1 June 2011|accessdate=7 September 2016}}.</ref> and a derivation from {{cog|sco|chad||[[river]] [[gravel]]}} stated in some dictionaries is now thought to be nothing more than guesswork.<ref>{{cite-book|author=William Safire|authorlink=William Safire|title=The Right Word in the Right Place at the Right Time: Wit and Wisdom from the Popular “On Language” Column in The New York Times Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Simon & Schuster}}|year=2004|page=43|isbn=978-0-7432-4244-8}}.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/tʃad/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/tʃæd/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|æd|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|chad|chads}}<ref>{{R:Macmillan|chad}}.</ref>

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Small pieces of [[paper]] [[punch]]ed out from the [[edge]]s of [[continuous]] [[stationery]], [[punched card]]s, [[paper tape]], etc.
#* {{quote-web|author=David P. Mikkelson|title=Chad: Does the word ‘chad’ come from the Chadless keypunch, invented by a Mr. Chadless?|url=http://www.snopes.com/business/names/chad.asp|archiveurl=<!--Cannot be archived by Archive.org as "[t]his URL has been excluded from the Wayback Machine"-->|archivedate=|work={{w|Snopes.com}}|date=1 June 2011|accessdate=7 September 2016|passage=The keypunch wasn't named after a Mr. Chadless; it was so named because, as expected, it punched tape while producing little or no '''chad'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} One of these pieces of paper.
#* {{quote-us-patent|author=Ross A. Lake|title=Printing Perforating Telegraph Apparatus|number=2255794|url=https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/pdfs/ad8227befa43e0ec83f0/US2255794.pdf|date=20 May 1939|passage=Prior devices of the type according to the present invention have been arranged to cut out the perforations completely at a single movement, thereby producing '''chads''' or waste material which often present difficult problems of disposal.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=J[ohn] W[illiam] Freebody|title=Telegraphy|location=London|publisher=[[w:Isaac Pitman|Isaac Pitman & Sons]]|year=1959|oclc=892562842|passage=The small hinged discs of paper, called ‘'''chad'''’, remain attached to the body of the tape.}}
#* {{quote-journal|author={{w|Supreme Court of the United States}}, [[per curiam]]|title=''{{w|Bush v. Gore}}''|journal={{w|United States Reports}}|date=12 December 2000|volume=531|page=98 at 105|passage=Much of the controversy seems to revolve around ballot cards designed to be perforated by a stylus but which, either through error or deliberate omission, have not been perforated with sufficient precision for a machine to count them. In some cases a piece of the card–a '''chad'''–is hanging, say by two corners. In other cases there is no separation at all, just an indentation.}}

